            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Spanish for the toast, then the Spanish for the honeymoon."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: casado",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "casado — \"married\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mi hermano está casado con una francesa. My brother is married to a Frenchwoman."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Estar casado con — to be married to."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "casado is built on the participle of casar 'to marry', which comes from casa 'house', inherited from Latin casa 'a hut' (Lewis & Short). To marry was to set up house."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
